{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 8656d9ee, 01d2c5f8 and dc56a29d for the cutover head\n(05d618e2). Your stop was right, and your numbers change my\nruling. An entry records no birth use. The scope retains the\nbirth, and the entry bytes stay as they were before a140a1c6.\n\nWhy this is sound. Each later use states `fact.at.genesis`, which\nis the entry hash of the first entry. `admitSequenceGenesis`\naccepts only canonical bytes that have that entry hash. So the\nentry already commits to the birth bytes. The `content` digest of\na birth use is a second digest of the same bytes, and it costs\nabout 362 bytes that the worst-case entry cannot hold.\n\nThe rule:\n1. Remove the birth uses from entries: `withBirthUses`,\n   `countedUses`, `entryUseLimit`, `birthsWithin`, and the birth\n   charge in the two v17 forecasts. `usesPerEntry` and the v17\n   entry-byte arithmetic return to their values of today.\n   destination@17 stays admitted. No entry vector changes for\n   this question.\n2. When a scope retains a later entry of a foreign sequence for\n   an origin, it also retains the first entry of that sequence.\n   The bytes come from the read already made. Keep the part of\n   a31fdb6d that carries the birth bytes through `Fetched` and\n   the judgment copies. One row holds one content digest.\n3. `RetainedOwn` gains one read: the retained birth row for a\n   `SequenceRef`. The scope keeps the association from (scope,\n   genesis) to the row in its own storage. `readRetainedUses`\n   classifies a later use as genuine when that read returns bytes\n   that `admitSequenceGenesis` accepts for `use.fact.at` and the\n   kind equals `use.kind`. The `heldFetched` rule does not change.\n4. A birth row lives while any held entry of the scope has a\n   later use of its sequence. `archive-local-references.ts` and\n   `archive-foreign.ts` derive that from the uses. An archive\n   carries the birth rows that its entries need.\n5. The retained-byte caps charge the birth bytes. Use the rule\n   that the scope applies today when two entries share one\n   retained body. State that rule and the measured numbers in the\n   note. Do not raise a cap.\n6. Replay and `dap verify` find a birth row by the same read. An\n   absent birth row gives the reason that an absent retained body\n   gives today.\n\nStop and tell me if this rule needs an added read, a change to\nentry bytes, or a storage change that you cannot bound in this\nhead. I then rule between option (b) and a larger `entryBytes`.\n\nWhat this retires. In a140a1c6, the sentence that an entry\nrecords a use of the first entry, and conditions 1, 2 and 6. In\n28295c20, points 1, 2 and 4. Conditions 3, 4 and 5 of a140a1c6\nhold for the retained bytes. Q-V2 still waits on this change.\n\nOption (d) is not needed for the bytes. Destination@17 only\nmarks a fact from another change scope as not sound. I take that\nquestion to the security requests after the cutover, not now.\n\nThe two faults that the lane is diagnosing stay with the lane:\nthe `creation.test.ts` RPC fault and the five v17 tests that\nfailed at de0d44ba. Tell me if either needs a ruling.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}